4373:
Well, it would be useful to have some more detail on the typing

procedure for these programs. For instance: this ":PRINT; LIST" is

literal? I mean, you actually need to type : before PRINT and ; before

LIST? What is the purpose?


The colon : is used to activate the interface used for tape I/O.
The PRINT command tells the program that this is an output.
Bally / Astrocades  use the semicolon as a command delimiter,
meaning tahe each command on a line is separated by a semicolon, much like your email addresses you are sending to are separated by a comma.
The LIST command tells the Bally to list out the program, which is waht you are wanting to send to the tape. There are variations but this is the thumbnail explaination.
As far as the special stuff on the Bally tape, This was a way to access only the program you wanted. Mike White probably still remembers the proper syntax for saving and retrieving this way.




If I can figure out the encoding of 300 baud tapes, it would be possible

to store the programs in a binary format, to be available for use with a

tool for regenerating the wave files. Perhaps, it would be possible,

too, to make a simple PC-based utility for typing them (this could help

to prevent Astrocade's keyboards wearing).



Ronaldo Goulart














